      "type": "text",
      "text": "{\"id\":\"AAMkADllY2NkMmU1LTc3YTEtNGRkMC1hMjBkLWU1NjIzOTY5OTZhMQBGAAAAAAC2MTk9L2QgQLGbcPk-hCdiBwDBpLwblpT0TZ_t-vz7Ao6sAAAAAAEMAADBpLwblpT0TZ_t-vz7Ao6sAAWN0g4TAAA=\",\"subject\":\"UBS: UBS Global I/O Semiconductors \\\"Foundry: Much larger N2 TAM and faster A14...\\\"\",\"bodyPreview\":\"See our updated analysis for N2 TAM, leading edge capacity, and mature foundry supply vs demand.\\r\\n                Global Research\\r\\n28 September 2026\\r\\nGlobal I/O      ●       UBS Global I/O Semiconductors\\r\\nFoundry: Much larger N2 TAM and faster A14 expansi\",\"body\":{\"contentType\":\"html\",\"content\":\"\\r\\nSee our updated analysis for N2 TAM, leading edge capacity, and mature foundry supply vs demand. <table class=\\\"wrap\\\"><tbody><tr></tr></tbody></table><table class=\\\"content\\\"><tbody><tr><td class=\\\"innerpadding\\\" style=\\\"padding:10px 30px 10px 30px\\\"><table><tbody><tr><td style=\\\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#000000;font-size:14px\\\"><a href=\\\"https://neo.ubs.com/r/7/?id=h7db02a42,b4801f6,4bf3043b\\\"> </a></td><td> </td><td class=\\\"english-font\\\" style=\\\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12px;color:#646464;letter-spacing:0;line-height:14px;text-align:right\\\">Global Research <br />28 September 2026 </td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr><td class=\\\"innerpadding\\\" style=\\\"padding:10px 30px 10px 30px\\\"><table><tbody><tr><td class=\\\"emailh2\\\" style=\\\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#000000;font-size:16px;font-weight:bold\\\"><table><tbody><tr><td style=\\\"color:#000000;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:0;line-height:18px\\\">Global I/O</td><td style=\\\"font-size:12px;padding:0px 5px 0px 5px\\\">●</td><td class=\\\"emailh2\\\" style=\\\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#898888 !important;font-size:16px;font-weight:bold\\\">UBS Global I/O Semiconductors</td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr><td class=\\\"innerpadding\\\" style=\\\"padding:10px 30px 10px 30px\\\"><table><tbody><tr><td class=\\\"headline\\\" style=\\\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#1C1C1C;font-size:38px;line-height:46px;letter-spacing:0\\\"><a href=\\\"https://neo.ubs.com/r/7/?id=h7db02a42,b4801f6,4bf3043c&amp;e=ZGlkPUFDNy1FLTE4OTI2ODQ3MCZvZmZfaWQ9QUM3LUUtMTU5MDcwMjE2MiZtYT1ZNDY3ODUzNDk0OTc4NUE0NSZjYW1wX2lkPUVNOlVOS1c6MjAyNi0wOToyNzpD&amp;s=hHkGrlw2h5IdYSXzIE1bGjazTujjnonfYK2cs9Pgzz8\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" style=\\\"color:#1C1C1C;text-decoration:none;font-weight:normal\\\">Foundry: Much larger N2 TAM and faster A14 expansion; mature foundry upcycle underway</a> </td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr><td class=\\\"innerpadding\\\" style=\\\"padding:10px 30px 10px 30px\\\"><table><tbody><tr><td class=\\\"bodycopy\\\" style=\\\"padding-bottom:10px;padding-left:0px;padding-right:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#1C1C1C;padding-top:10px;font-size:16px;line-height:22px;letter-spacing:0\\\">See our updated analysis for N2 TAM, leading edge capacity, and mature foundry supply vs demand. <br /><br /><table><tbody><tr><td><table><tbody><tr><td style=\\\"padding-bottom:10px;padding-left:18px;padding-right:18px;padding-top:12px;border-radius:3px;-webkit-border-radius:3px\\\"><a href=\\\"https://neo.ubs.com/r/7/?id=h7db02a42,b4801f6,4bf3043d&amp;e=ZGlkPUFDNy1FLTE4OTI2ODQ3MCZvZmZfaWQ9QUM3LUUtMTU5MDcwMjE2MyZtYT1ZNDY3ODUzNDk0OTc4NUE0NSZjYW1wX2lkPUVNOlVOS1c6MjAyNi0wOToyNzpD&amp;s=cvKqiVqd7nppd_Xt9h_dVWavFDfbTouYxE-83wXaIl0\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" style=\\\"display:inline-block;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#ffffff;font-size:16px;font-weight:normal;text-decoration:none\\\">Read Report   &gt; </a></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr><td class=\\\"innerpadding\\\" style=\\\"padding:10px 30px 10px 30px\\\"><table><tbody><tr><td class=\\\"bodycopy\\\" style=\\\"padding-bottom:20px;padding-left:0px;padding-right:0px;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#000000;padding-top:0px;font-size:12px;line-height:16px\\\"><p style=\\\"margin-bottom:10px\\\">N2 demand inflection larger than expected </p><p style=\\\"margin-top:0;color:#1C1C1C;font-size:16px;letter-spacing:0;line-height:22px\\\">Our proprietary bottoms-up N2 foundry TAM analysis points to substantially larger demand of 320kwpm by 2028E, versus our prior estimate of approximately 230kwpm a year ago. The increase is driven primarily by stronger server CPU demand and accelerating AI compute deployments, particularly as a growing number of ASIC programs ramp into volume production alongside GPUs over the next several years. At the same time, we expect Intel to migrate a larger portion of its PC CPU production to 18A as execution improves. As a result, we estimate server CPUs and accelerators will account for 54% of N2 foundry demand by 2028, up from our previous estimate of 44%, while PCs should contribute 19% and smartphones/tablets 26%. (See Quantifying N2 demand opportunities for foundry -N2 foundry demand by application (2028E)). The larger N2 opportunity supports robust revenue growth for TSMC through 2028-29E as the industry's major foundry supplier, even as competition from Samsung Foundry, Intel, and Terafab increases modestly.</p><p style=\\\"margin-bottom:10px\\\">Further acceleration of N3/2 expansion; quicker A14 capacity build-up </p><p style=\\\"margin-top:0;color:#1C1C1C;font-size:16px;letter-spacing:0;line-height:22px\\\">To meet the larger N2 demand outlook, we raise our estimate for industry N2 capacity at end-2028 to 312kwpm, up from 230kwpm previously. We forecast TSMC's N2 capacity reaching 210kwpm in 2028, versus our prior estimate of 160kwpm. Intel should also scale capacity more aggressively, exceeding 60kwpm in 2028E for internal CPU production, compared with our previous forecast of 40kwpm. Meanwhile, strengthening cloud AI demand in 2027-28E is pushing our industry N3 capacity forecast to 290-300kwpm by 2028, up from 270kwpm previously, driven entirely by faster expansion at TSMC. TSMC's A14 node remains on track for mass production in 2028, supported by steady R&amp;D progress and growing customer engagement. Industry feedback suggests TSMC is pulling forward its expansion schedule, targeting approximately 60kwpm of A14 capacity in 2028 when the node enters mass production, based on UBSe. Reflecting the more aggressive leading-edge expansion, we raise our TSMC capex forecast to US$90bn in 2027 and US$105bn in 2028, up from US$63bn this year. See our <a href=\\\"https://neo.ubs.com/r/7/?id=h7db02a42,b4801f6,4bf3043e&amp;e=ZGlkPUFDNy1FLTE4OTI2ODQ3MCZvZmZfaWQ9QUM3LUUtMTU5MDcwMjE2NCZtYT1ZNDY3ODUzNDk0OTc4NUE0NSZjYW1wX2lkPUVNOlVOS1c6MjAyNi0wOToyNzpD&amp;s=Isrugqb4GNED-P4-AgdLHPe_rf616KV_fJQPEz_4qAM\\\">Q326 earnings preview</a>. </p><p style=\\\"margin-bottom:10px\\\">Mature foundry: favorable supply/demand and pricing outlook in 2027-28 </p><p style=\\\"margin-top:0;color:#1C1C1C;font-size:16px;letter-spacing:0;line-height:22px\\\">We were early in advocating the anti-consensus view of a mature foundry upcycle, driven by an industry-wide supply and resource reallocation. Utilization and pricing trends have continued to improve throughout 2026, and we expect more meaningful earnings upside in 2027-28. Our updated analysis suggests 8-inch foundry utilization could rise to 95% in 2027, up from 85% in 2026, supported by growing server PMIC demand. While 12-inch 28/40nm demand may face near-term headwinds from weaker smartphone and consumer demand, we still expect utilization to improve to 88% in 2027 from 84% in 2026, supported by modest capacity growth. We also believe competitive behaviour from Chinese foundries should remain rational into 2027, as major players increasingly prioritize capital allocation toward leading-edge technologies.</p><p style=\\\"margin-bottom:10px\\\">Stock recommendations </p><p style=\\\"margin-top:0;color:#1C1C1C;font-size:16px;letter-spacing:0;line-height:22px\\\">We reiterate Buy on TSMC and expect further upward revisions to Street revenue, EPS and capex estimates for 2027-28. Within mature foundry, UMC remains our top pick, as it should be a major beneficiary of overflow from larger foundries, with additional upside from silicon photonics and advanced packaging. We also like PSMC and SMIC. We remain Neutral on GlobalFoundries, Vanguard and Hua Hong. See <a>Figure 1</a> for valuation details. </p></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr><td class=\\\"innerpadding\\\" style=\\\"padding:10px 30px 10px 30px\\\"><table style=\\\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#333333;width:100%;font-size:14px;text-align:left\\\"><tbody><tr><td><a href=\\\"mailto:sunny.lin@ubs.com?subject=UBS Research follow-up: Foundry%3A%20Much%20larger%20N2%20TAM%20and%20faster%20A14%20expansion%3B%20mature%20foundry%20upcycle%20underway\\\" class=\\\"appleLinksBlack\\\" style=\\\"color:#898888;text-decoration:none;line-height:20px\\\"> </a></td><td class=\\\"bodycopy english-font\\\" style=\\\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;padding-top:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-right:0;padding-left:10px;line-height:25px;color:#898888\\\"><a href=\\\"mailto:sunny.lin@ubs.com?subject=UBS Research follow-up: Foundry%3A%20Much%20larger%20N2%20TAM%20and%20faster%20A14%20expansion%3B%20mature%20foundry%20upcycle%20underway\\\" class=\\\"appleLinksBlack\\\" style=\\\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#898888;text-decoration:none;line-height:20px\\\">Sunny Lin, Analyst</a> <br /><a href=\\\"mailto:sunny.lin@ubs.com?subject=UBS Research follow-up: Foundry%3A%20Much%20larger%20N2%20TAM%20and%20faster%20A14%20expansion%3B%20mature%20foundry%20upcycle%20underway\\\" class=\\\"appleLinksBlack\\\" style=\\\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#0C7EEC6;text-decoration:none;line-height:16px;font-size:14px\\\">sunny.lin@ubs.com</a> <br /><a class=\\\"appleLinksBlack\\\" style=\